<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oriental Institute</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1919</span> · Also known as Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oriental Institute of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>, renamed the Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures in 2023, is a research centre and museum for the archaeology and languages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>, founded in 1919 by James Henry Breasted. Its expeditions excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> and the Diyala sites, including Tell Asmar, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, and it produced the Chicago Assyrian Dictionary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oriental-institute.jpg" alt="Oriental Institute" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Salsb at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orientalinstitutedoors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
